ith H^1.98 ± 0.27 in 178 men (MRI). This is why plain FFMI is roughly flat with height among humans. A body scaled up at the same shape gains FFM with H^3, so its plain FFMI grows in proportion to H.\n- **Big bodies change shape too.** Galileo, Haldane, McMahon 1973 and Biewener 2005 all say real bodies change proportions as size grows.\n\nDerived checks (my own arithmetic):\n- At 1.80 m, 1.8·FFM/H^3 equals plain FFMI exactly.\n- For the same shape at 3.048 m, plain FFMI is 1.693 times larger and the rescaled index is unchanged. Rex's plain FFMI of 269 becomes 159 (tail included).\n- Kouri's correction applied at 3.048 m gives about 261, not 159, so it fails there.\n- In ANSUR II men, BMI is flat with height, but 1.8·W/H^3 falls as 1/H. The tallest tenth (median 1.870 m) scores about 12% below the shortest tenth (median 1.647 m) at the same median BMI.\n- The meatball check reproduces 924 (899–949 for 5–10% fat).\n\nRecommendation: keep S = 1.8 × FFM / H^3, with its limits stated.",
