 "summary": "I found sources for all three functional limits, but no source gives the limits themselves. Each threshold below is my own estimate, built from sourced pieces.\n\n(1) Soft-tissue contact limits flexion. CDC's goniometry protocol says elbow flexion ends \"usually when the muscle bulk of the anterior forearm contacts the muscle bulk of the anterior upper arm\", and knee flexion ends when calf meets thigh. Normal passive elbow flexion in men aged 20-44 is 144.6 deg (CDC); the VA standard is 0-145 deg (38 CFR 4.71). Zwerus et al. measured active flexion in men falling with BMI: 151, 145, 140 and 136 deg across the four BMI groups. An open-access review says \"bodybuilders may flex only up to 130 degrees due to their muscle bulk\"; it cites a textbook and gives no measured sample.\n\n(2) Hand-to-mouth needs about 130 deg in natural movement (Morrey 1981: most daily tasks 30-130; Safaee-Rad 1990: feeding 70-130; Murphy 2006: drinking 136). With compensation, 49 of 50 adults did all 12 daily tasks with flexion limited to 120 deg (Vasen 1995). The VA rates flexion limited to 110 deg as 0% and 100 deg as 10%.\n\n(3) Extra tissue reduces range of motion. In obese men, 9 of 30 motions were reduced, including shoulder adduction (by up to 39%) and knee flexion (Park 2010). Shoulder range is lower in bodybuilders and powerlifters. Training alone does not reduce range of motion (Afonso 2021 meta-analysis). Chang 1988 is abstract-only and gives no joint detail.\n\n(4) ANSUR II measures standing \"heels together as much as possible\", with \"elbows lightly touching the sides\". The handbook adds: \"On individuals with muscular upper arms, the elbows may not touch the sides.\" Thigh girth is taken with the legs apart \"just enough so that the thighs do not touch\". Obese men walk with steps 0.16 m wide against 0.08 m for non-obese men, attributed to fat inside the thighs (Spyropoulos 1991).\n\n(5) I found no published model that predicts maximum flexion from limb girth. Thigh-calf contact starts above 130 deg of knee flexion and scales with girth (Zelle 2007, 2009). Animation and digital-human models handle tissue collision but take joint angles as inputs.\n\nI derived calibration numbers from the ANSUR II CSV and simple geometry (all estimates):\n- With the legs vertical, the median man has a 4.4 cm gap between his thighs just above the knee; 1 of 4,082 men has none.\n- An arm angle derived from seated forearm-forearm breadth has a median of about 10 deg, 99th percentile 19 deg and maximum 26 deg.\n- For hand-to-mouth, geometry with median proportions needs about 123 deg of elbow flexion with a straight wrist, or about 99 deg with the wrist fully flexed.\n- A wedge model calibrated to 145 deg puts flexion at 100 deg when arm girth is 2.7 times the normal-weight reference (pure geometry), or 1.8 times if the steeper loss seen with BMI is applied.\n\nScripts are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-rex-strength-lab/9b81b796-3162-56bd-85b7-22e02b6583e2/scratchpad/research/functional-limits/scripts/ and raw downloads in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-rex-strength-lab/9b81b796-3162-56bd-85b7-22e02b6583e2/scratchpad/research/functional-limits/dl/. I relied on these existing builds.json keys rather than re-researching them: ansur2_dataset, ansur2_segments_male, ansur2_measurement_definitions, ansur2_thigh, ansur2_biceps_flexed, ansur2_forearm_flexed.",
