 "recommendations": "Label every threshold below as an estimate on the page. All three use 2D outline contact, which the sources support as the real mechanism (CDC protocol; Zwerus; Park 2010).\n\n0. Implementation rule (essential). If the whole picture is stretched sideways about the body's midline, every gap (arm to torso, thigh to thigh) stretches too and never closes, so no limit can ever trip. Keep the skeleton fixed: shoulder joint centres, hip joint centres, elbow axes and leg axes scale with height only. Widen each segment's outline about its own axis: torso about the midline, each arm about the arm axis, each thigh about its leg axis.\n   - Shoulder joint: about 2 cm medial and 4 cm below the acromion. The acromion comes from ANSUR biacromial breadth (0.236 H) and acromial height (0.821 H).\n   - Hip joint centres: about 0.087 m either side of the midline for a 1.755 m man (about 0.05 H each side), from Hara 2016 (8 mm + 0.086 x leg length).\n   - Compute every limit on two or three base shapes (the ANSUR median template, one bodybuilder-proportion template, and the duo's traced outlines). Mark each limit as a band, not a line.\n\n(a) Arms can no longer hang by the sides.\n   - Test: the arm straight, palm toward the thigh, rotated in the front view about the fixed shoulder joint. θ_rest is the smallest angle from vertical at which the outline of the upper arm (below chest height, about 0.735 H), forearm and hand clears the torso, hip and thigh outline. Skip the zone just under the armpit: arm and torso are joined there by the axillary folds.\n   - Threshold: θ_rest ≥ 30° (estimate; sensitivity band 20-45°). Reasons:\n     - ANSUR's standard posture has 'elbows lightly touching the sides'. The model gives 4-6° for the median man.\n     - The handbook notes that 'muscular upper arms' already keep the elbows off the sides, so small angles are ordinary human variation.\n     - A seated arm angle derived from 4,082 soldiers has median 10°, 99th percentile 19° and maximum 26°, so 30° is beyond every one of them.\n     - 30° is the low end of the art spec's A-pose: at the limit the figure cannot relax out of an A-pose.\n   - Ignores: 3D (arms can hang slightly in front of the lats); tissue compression of several cm; shoulder-girdle shrug or protraction moving the joint; clothing; the test-region choice (moves the crossing between width x1.3 and x1.65 for the median man).\n\n(b) Thighs touch all the way to the knee.\n   - Test: legs straight and vertical, ankle centres under the hip joint centres (0° hip abduction). The inner thigh outlines overlap at every level from the crotch (0.481 H) down to the top of the kneecap (about knee height midpatella + 4 cm, about 0.30 H).\n   - Calibration: for the median ANSUR man the gap just above the knee is about 4.4 cm; only 1 of 4,082 men has none; the lower-thigh width must grow about 1.34x (1.17-1.53 from 5th to 95th percentile) to close it.\n   - Plain-language meaning: the knees cannot come closer than the hip-joint spacing (about 0.17 m), so every step must be at least that wide. That is about twice the 0.08 m of non-obese men and like the 0.16 m measured in obese men (Spyropoulos 1991), and the thighs rub with each step.\n   - State the stance on the page. With heels together (ANSUR) many ordinary men's knees already meet; with feet shoulder-width apart the limit moves to about x2.2 thigh width.\n   - Ignores: non-circular thighs and medial bulk; compression; knee alignment; gait strategies such as swinging the leg around.\n\n(c) Hands can no longer reach the face. Two steps.\n   - Step 1, maximum elbow flexion from soft-tissue contact: tan((180° - φ_max)/2) = 0.315 x k. Here k is upper-arm plus forearm girth (or front-view width, assuming circular sections) relative to normal-weight men (ANSUR II BMI 18.5-25: flexed biceps 0.324 m + flexed forearm 0.290 m). k=1 gives 145°, matching CDC, Zwerus and 38 CFR. Or run the same contact test directly on the arm outlines, calibrated so the reference arm gives 145°.\n   - Step 2, compare with the flexion the figure needs to reach its mouth, computed from its own segment lengths and head position. For human proportions that is about 123° with a straight wrist and about 99° with the wrist flexed to its 80° limit.\n   - Threshold: φ_max ≤ 100° (estimate; band 95-110°): even the fingertips, with the wrist fully flexed and the head upright, cannot touch the mouth. This agrees with the VA schedule rating 110° as 0% and 100° as the first rated loss.\n   - Optionally mark a softer 'eating and drinking need compensation' line at φ_max of about 120°: Vasen 1995 found all 12 daily tasks still done with flexion braced at 120°; natural feeding uses about 130° (Morrey 1981, Safaee-Rad 1990).\n   - Model crossing: φ_max = 100° at k ≈ 2.7 (pure geometry, the recommended main model, consistent with the review's ~130° for bodybuilders) or k ≈ 1.8 (steeper loss fitted to BMI data, a pessimistic bound). Show the gap between them as the uncertainty.\n   - Ignores: 3D shoulder, neck and trunk compensation; chest and deltoid bulk blocking the arm from crossing the body; biceps vs triceps distribution; muscle vs fat compliance; forearm rotation.\n   - For Rex and Ollie, use their own traced proportions (snout and muzzle position); the human 99° does not apply to them.\n\nRough order (uniform widening of the median man, at fixed height mass ∝ width²): (b) first at about x1.3 width, about 1.7x the reference score; then (a) at x1.3-1.65, about 1.7-2.7x; then (c) at x1.8-2.7, about 3-7x. That puts all three limits between the top human anchors and the heaviest comic characters, far below the meatball. Real builds are not uniform (bodybuilders have small waists and huge arms), so compute on each base silhouette.\n\nPage wording: 'tissue meets tissue' as the cause (sourced); every threshold is an estimate, with its reason; past width x1.2 everything is extrapolation; say what each model ignores.",
 "gaps": "Not fetched or not verified:\n- Norkin & White 'Measurement of Joint Motion', Kapandji, Magee and the AAOS 'Joint Motion' booklet. No primary text was accessible: Google Books API quota exhausted, publisher pages only. Their exact end-feel wording and the AAOS 150° norm are not cited. The soft-tissue end point rests on the CDC protocol, Zwerus 2019 and Islam 2020 instead.\n- Morrey 1981 full text (JBJS paywall, HTTP 402). Per-task angles (e.g. drinking 72-129°) are quoted second-hand everywhere and were not used; only the abstract (30-130°, occiput 140°) is.\n- Chang, Buschbacher & Edlich 1988 full text (SAGE 403). Which joints were limited in power lifters, and by how much, is unknown; the abstract mentions no joint and no elbow or adduction values.\n- Park 2010 per-motion table, including how shoulder adduction was measured and its baseline. Abstract only.\n- Vasen 1995: which 12 tasks, and what failed at 105° or below. Abstract only.\n- Oosterwijk 2018 per-task angles. Abstract only.\n- Aizawa 2010 and Magermans 2005 (face-washing and hair-combing angles). Paywalled; abstracts give no angles.\n- Harrington 2007's exact hip-joint equation (seen only second-hand). Hara 2016 used instead.\n- The Hara 2016 formulas and the Zwerus Table 4 values were read through a fetch tool's rendering of the PMC page (curl was CAPTCHA-blocked; the equations are images). The Zwerus abstract was cross-checked via the Europe PMC API.\n- The CDC public ROM dataset (with height and weight), which could test BMI against flexion directly: the download link is dead (404) and the Wayback Machine was unreachable through the proxy.\n\nNot found at all:\n- Any measurement of elbow flexion or shoulder adduction in bodybuilders or strongmen. The only statement is a narrative review citing a textbook, '130 degrees'.\n- Any study of thigh contact or 'thigh gap' in men. The only hits are aesthetic surveys of women's thigh gaps.\n- Any model in biomechanics, ergonomics (RAMSIS, Jack) or animation that outputs maximum flexion from limb cross-section.\n\nAssumptions in my derived numbers:\n- Shoulder joint 2 cm medial and 4 cm below the acromion.\n- Mouth 0.19/0.17/0.11 m from the shoulder joint.\n- ASIS-based leg length estimated from iliocristale height.\n- Circular limb sections.\n- Flexed girths as a stand-in for relaxed tissue thickness.\n- Dutch BMI groups mapped onto ANSUR girths.\n\nAll are estimates; sensitivity was checked only for some of them (upper-arm length and mouth position: ±3°; shoulder-joint offset: ±1-2° on the seated arm angle; test region for the arm-hang angle: crossing at width x1.3-1.65). Nothing above about 1.2x the reference arm or thigh girth is measured, so all limit positions are extrapolations.\n\nHousekeeping: early in this run I accidentally wrote 14 PubMed abstract .txt files into /home/user/rex-strength-lab/build-comparison/. I moved 13 to the scratchpad dl/ folder and deleted the 14th after checking it was identical to the scratchpad copy. Nothing of mine remains in the repo; the other uncommitted changes in git status are not mine."
}

Project: "Build Comparison", a chart that ranks male body builds by "muscle level" irrespective of height, from ordinary men through the limits of human possibility to the monstrous (comic characters, and two original 3 m characters, Rex and Ollie). Read /home/user/rex-strength-lab/build-comparison/BRIEF.md first (10 minutes): it states the decisions already made and the problems to solve.
The working score is FFMI after rescaling the body to 1.80 m at the same shape: S = 1.8 * FFM / H^3 (FFM = fat-free mass in kg, H = height in m), shown beside plain FFMI = FFM / H^2. A rough first pass gives: US Army median man about 24, Ronnie Coleman about 35-39, Eddie Hall about 38, the heaviest comic characters 57-81, Rex about 141 (tail excluded, height to crest). A solid ball of muscle as tall as the figure scores about 924.
The sister project /home/user/rex-strength-lab/build-calculator/ already holds 55 builds and 139 verified facts in data/builds.json (read its "facts" keys and labels before you start; do NOT re-research a fact that already exists there unless the brief needs a number it lacks, and say so when you rely on an existing key). ANSUR II CSVs are in /home/user/rex-strength-lab/build-calculator/data/ansur2/ (ANSUR_II_MALE_Public.csv, 4,082 men; values in mm and kg x10? check: weightkg is in tenths of kg, lengths in mm).
