 "summary": "Official Marvel sources describe the body makeup of several characters, and the chart can use some of it. Nothing gives a body-fat percentage or a tissue density in kg/m3. Key findings:\n\n(1) Asgardian density is official. The Official Handbook of the Marvel Universe, Deluxe Edition (1985-86), as reprinted in the 2006 Essential volume 1, says: \"Asgardian flesh and bone is about three times denser than similar human tissue, contributing to the gods' superhuman strength and weight.\" The 1987 trade paperback vol 4 and The Definitive Thor (Panini, 2011) have the same wording. DK's Marvel Encyclopedia (2014 and 2019) says \"Their skin and bones are three times as dense as those of a human ... due to their density, weigh far more than humans of comparable size.\" Thor's 640 lb divided by 3 is about 97 kg (213 lb), which puts his score at an ordinary level (S about 20-22, against 60-67 as listed).\n\n(2) Wolverine's adamantium is official: Marvel.com says it \"adds an extra 105 pounds to his frame—or 47.6 kilograms\" (300 lb with it, 195 lb without). I found no handbook figure, and \"nearly 100 pounds\" appears only in fan-wiki text.\n\n(3) Colossus, armoured: the handbook calls the substance \"of unknown composition but ... analogous to osmium and to carbon steel\" and says he gains \"additional mass from an unknown, perhaps extradimensional source\". His body is not tissue, so he is not comparable.\n\n(4) The Thing: the official wording is a rock-like or rocky skin, hide or exterior (\"supple\"). Nothing gives its mass.\n\n(5) Hulk and Abomination: the handbook says gamma radiation \"fortified his cellular structure and added, from some as yet unknown source, over 800 pounds of bone marrow and tissue\" (Hulk) or \"bone marrow and muscle tissue\" (Abomination). So the added mass is officially tissue.\n\n(6) Luke Cage: \"very dense muscle and bone tissue, and steel-hard skin\" (DK 2019) and \"Superdense Body Mass\" (Marvel.com), with no factor.\n\n(7) Kingpin: \"His great bulk does not consist of fat but of muscles ... as in a sumo wrestler\" (handbook), and \"Most of Fisk's bulk is actually muscle, not fat\" (Marvel.com). The \"9 pounds of fat\" figure is fan-wiki only.\n\n(8) For Superman, Batman, Darkseid, Doomsday and Bane, the DC sources (two DK books) say nothing about density or composition. Bane's Venom is described as a super-steroid. Density claims for Superman, Darkseid and Doomsday, and Black Panther's \"denser tissue\", come only from fan wikis.\n\n(9) Body-fat percentage: none in any Marvel or DC source searched, as expected. The only statements are in words: Kingpin, and Angel's \"body is virtually devoid of fat\".",
